WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.714
Welcome. Tool use turns generated intent
into action, so this

2
00:00:05.714 --> 00:00:11.429
class focuses on delegated authority
rather than novelty. You will

3
00:00:11.429 --> 00:00:17.143
define what one tool can read or change,
expose consequential

4
00:00:17.143 --> 00:00:22.857
parameters before execution, verify the
real postcondition, and recover from

5
00:00:22.857 --> 00:00:28.571
timeouts, duplicate calls, partial
effects, and untrusted output. The goal

6
00:00:28.571 --> 00:00:34.286
is a workflow in which people can see,
approve, verify,

7
00:00:34.286 --> 00:00:40.000
cancel, and recover from consequence.

8
00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:46.429
Begin with a tool contract. Give the tool
one clear

9
00:00:46.429 --> 00:00:52.857
purpose. Define typed inputs, permitted
values, size and format limits,

10
00:00:52.857 --> 00:00:59.286
required identifiers, and validation
failures. Define success, error, and

11
00:00:59.286 --> 00:01:05.714
unknown-outcome responses. Inventory every
system and data class the tool

12
00:01:05.714 --> 00:01:12.143
reads, writes, sends, spends, or deletes.
Name side effects such

13
00:01:12.143 --> 00:01:18.571
as a message, charge, deployment,
permission change, record update, or

14
00:01:18.571 --> 00:01:25.000
external disclosure. Record authentication
and least-privilege requirements,

15
00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:31.429
call-rate and cost ceilings, timeouts, and
concurrency limits. Decide whether

16
00:01:31.429 --> 00:01:37.857
the action is safe to retry. If it writes,

17
00:01:37.857 --> 00:01:44.286
identify an idempotency key or state that
it is not

18
00:01:44.286 --> 00:01:50.714
retry-safe. Finally, name the required
approval, postcondition check, audit

19
00:01:50.714 --> 00:01:57.143
fields, compensation, rollback, and
escalation path. A capability description

20
00:01:57.143 --> 00:02:03.571
says what a tool can do. A contract says

21
00:02:03.571 --> 00:02:10.000
when, where, and under whose authority it
may do it.

22
00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:15.000
Consider a tool that sends a customer
status message. Its

23
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:20.000
purpose is one outbound message to one
resolved recipient after

24
00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:25.000
review. Inputs include the recipient
identifier, approved channel, subject,

25
00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:30.000
body, incident reference, and idempotency
key. It reads approved contact

26
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:35.000
data and writes to the messaging service.
Side effects include

27
00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:40.000
external communication, possible
disclosure, and provider cost. The model

28
00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:45.000
may draft text, but it cannot choose an
ambiguous recipient,

29
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:50.000
widen the audience, or invent incident
facts. Before approval, the

30
00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:55.000
interface shows the resolved recipient,
exact message, channel, and expected

31
00:02:55.000 --> 00:03:00.000
send. Approval binds to those values for
one attempt. Verification

32
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:05.000
checks the provider record and message
identifier, not the model's

33
00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:10.000
statement that it sent successfully.
Recovery distinguishes confirmed failure

34
00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:15.000
from an unknown result after timeout.

35
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:20.667
Resolve intent before execution. Translate
the request into the exact

36
00:03:20.667 --> 00:03:26.333
tool, target, action, and material
parameters. Reject missing or ambiguous

37
00:03:26.333 --> 00:03:32.000
identifiers instead of guessing. Validate
values against the contract, then

38
00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:37.667
compare consequence with the current
approval tier. Read-only inspection,

39
00:03:37.667 --> 00:03:43.333
reversible internal writes, external
communication, spending, permission

40
00:03:43.333 --> 00:03:49.000
changes, and destructive action should not
share one blanket permission.

41
00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:54.667
Before a consequential call, show the
person the resolved target,

42
00:03:54.667 --> 00:04:00.333
operation, important values, expected
effect, and recovery boundary. Apply

43
00:04:00.333 --> 00:04:06.000
count, time, cost, and concurrency
ceilings in code rather than

44
00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:11.667
relying on polite instructions. Treat
returned text and files as

45
00:04:11.667 --> 00:04:17.333
untrusted data. A webpage, document,
issue, or tool response may

46
00:04:17.333 --> 00:04:23.000
contain malicious directions asking the
system to ignore policy, reveal

47
00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:28.667
secrets, change tools, or contact another
target. Those words remain

48
00:04:28.667 --> 00:04:34.333
data inside the task; they do not become
governing authority.

49
00:04:34.333 --> 00:04:40.000
Stop when the call would exceed scope or
approval.

50
00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:46.250
Choose one tool-enabled task. List the
exact target, action, material

51
00:04:46.250 --> 00:04:52.500
parameters, visible side effects, and
recovery limit a person must

52
00:04:52.500 --> 00:04:58.750
see before approving it. Name one value
the workflow must

53
00:04:58.750 --> 00:05:05.000
never guess.

54
00:06:35.000 --> 00:06:41.000
A returned success value is not the same
as the

55
00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:47.000
intended postcondition. Define what must
be true in the target

56
00:06:47.000 --> 00:06:53.000
system and check it independently. After
creating a record, confirm

57
00:06:53.000 --> 00:06:59.000
that exactly one record exists with the
expected values. After

58
00:06:59.000 --> 00:07:05.000
writing a file, compare location, size,
and digest. After deployment,

59
00:07:05.000 --> 00:07:11.000
check the deployed version, health, and a
representative behavior. After

60
00:07:11.000 --> 00:07:17.000
sending a message, reconcile the
provider's delivery record with the

61
00:07:17.000 --> 00:07:23.000
approved recipient and idempotency key.
Verification should also detect forbidden

62
00:07:23.000 --> 00:07:29.000
effects: no second record, no widened
audience, no unapproved permission,

63
00:07:29.000 --> 00:07:35.000
and no unrelated file change. Create an
audit record containing

64
00:07:35.000 --> 00:07:41.000
the request identity, authorization
decision, resolved target, tool and

65
00:07:41.000 --> 00:07:47.000
contract version, material parameters or
safe digest, start and completion

66
00:07:47.000 --> 00:07:53.000
time, result, postcondition evidence, and
recovery disposition. Do not log

67
00:07:53.000 --> 00:07:59.000
credentials, tokens, full private content,
or unnecessary personal data. If

68
00:07:59.000 --> 00:08:05.000
the postcondition cannot be checked,
report uncertainty rather than success.

69
00:08:05.000 --> 00:08:11.000
Checkpoint. A payment tool times out after
submitting a request.

70
00:08:11.000 --> 00:08:17.000
The model says the call failed and
proposes sending it

71
00:08:17.000 --> 00:08:23.000
again with the same amount and recipient
but no idempotency

72
00:08:23.000 --> 00:08:29.000
key. What is the outcome state, and what
should happen

73
00:08:29.000 --> 00:08:35.000
next?

74
00:09:35.000 --> 00:09:38.636
The outcome is unknown, not confirmed
failure. The external service

75
00:09:38.636 --> 00:09:42.273
may have accepted the request before the
response was lost.

76
00:09:42.273 --> 00:09:45.909
Stop the blind retry. Reconcile the
provider using the original

77
00:09:45.909 --> 00:09:49.545
safe request identity, recipient, amount,
and time window without exposing

78
00:09:49.545 --> 00:09:53.182
payment secrets. If one matching payment
exists, record success and

79
00:09:53.182 --> 00:09:56.818
do not repeat it. If confirmed absent and
the authorized

80
00:09:56.818 --> 00:10:00.455
workflow permits a new attempt, create a
new controlled request.

81
00:10:00.455 --> 00:10:04.091
If state cannot be established, escalate.
If you chose immediate

82
00:10:04.091 --> 00:10:07.727
retry, revise the rule: transport failure
does not prove business

83
00:10:07.727 --> 00:10:11.364
failure. Consequential writes need
idempotency and target-state verification

84
00:10:11.364 --> 00:10:15.000
before repetition.

85
00:10:15.000 --> 00:10:20.333
Plan recovery for partial and repeated
execution. Give retryable writes

86
00:10:20.333 --> 00:10:25.667
stable idempotency keys and store enough
state to recognize the

87
00:10:25.667 --> 00:10:31.000
same intended action. Separate confirmed
failure, confirmed success, and unknown

88
00:10:31.000 --> 00:10:36.333
outcome. In a multi-step workflow, record
each completed effect before

89
00:10:36.333 --> 00:10:41.667
starting the next dependent action. When a
later step fails,

90
00:10:41.667 --> 00:10:47.000
stop the sequence, inspect what already
changed, and decide whether

91
00:10:47.000 --> 00:10:52.333
to resume, compensate, roll back, or
escalate. Compensation is a

92
00:10:52.333 --> 00:10:57.667
new consequential action, not magic
reversal, so it needs its

93
00:10:57.667 --> 00:11:03.000
own contract and authority. Never assume
deletion recreates the prior

94
00:11:03.000 --> 00:11:08.333
state or that a refund erases disclosure.
Preserve the original

95
00:11:08.333 --> 00:11:13.667
request, approval, result, and recovery
chain. Use bounded retries with

96
00:11:13.667 --> 00:11:19.000
backoff only for errors the contract
identifies as transient. Do

97
00:11:19.000 --> 00:11:24.333
not retry invalid input, denied
permission, exceeded limits, destructive

98
00:11:24.333 --> 00:11:29.667
ambiguity, or untrusted instructions.
Disable or narrow the tool when

99
00:11:29.667 --> 00:11:35.000
observed behavior exceeds its tested
boundary.

100
00:11:35.000 --> 00:11:40.500
Imagine a deployment workflow that uploads
an artifact, updates configuration,

101
00:11:40.500 --> 00:11:46.000
and shifts traffic. Upload succeeds,
configuration succeeds, and the traffic

102
00:11:46.000 --> 00:11:51.500
check fails before the shift. The record
shows that no

103
00:11:51.500 --> 00:11:57.000
traffic moved, so repeating the entire
workflow could create unnecessary

104
00:11:57.000 --> 00:12:02.500
versions or overwrite reviewed
configuration. The bounded response

105
00:12:02.500 --> 00:12:08.000
verifies the artifact digest and
configuration, preserves those completed

106
00:12:08.000 --> 00:12:13.500
effects, repairs only the failed health
condition, reruns the check,

107
00:12:13.500 --> 00:12:19.000
and requests the exact traffic-shift
approval when ready. If the

108
00:12:19.000 --> 00:12:24.500
artifact or configuration differs from
approval, stop and roll back

109
00:12:24.500 --> 00:12:30.000
according to the contract. Step-level
evidence makes safe resumption possible.

110
00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:35.000
Keep people in control of consequence.
Require exact, current approval

111
00:12:35.000 --> 00:12:40.000
for destructive, external, costly,
privacy-sensitive, or permission-expanding

112
00:12:40.000 --> 00:12:45.000
actions. Approval must bind to the
resolved target, action, values,

113
00:12:45.000 --> 00:12:50.000
scope, and time; it is not a reusable
blank check.

114
00:12:50.000 --> 00:12:55.000
Provide preview and cancellation before
execution when consequence permits. Make

115
00:12:55.000 --> 00:13:00.000
denials fail closed. Separate the model
that proposes an action

116
00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:05.000
from deterministic validation and, for
high consequence, accountable human

117
00:13:05.000 --> 00:13:10.000
authorization. Restrict credentials to the
smallest tool and task boundary.

118
00:13:10.000 --> 00:13:15.000
Redact sensitive material from previews
and audit without hiding what

119
00:13:15.000 --> 00:13:20.000
is being approved. Provide status,
postcondition evidence, recovery options,

120
00:13:20.000 --> 00:13:25.000
and a clear handoff when automation stops.
Test duplicate requests,

121
00:13:25.000 --> 00:13:30.000
timeouts after success, partial
completion, stale state, target

122
00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:35.000
substitution, permission denial, limit
exhaustion, and malicious instructions

123
00:13:35.000 --> 00:13:40.000
returned by tools. A useful agent can do
less than

124
00:13:40.000 --> 00:13:45.000
its environment technically permits
because reliable authority is deliberately

125
00:13:45.000 --> 00:13:50.000
narrower than capability.

126
00:13:50.000 --> 00:13:55.625
Open the safe tool plan activity. Choose
one tool-enabled task

127
00:13:55.625 --> 00:14:01.250
and complete its purpose, inputs, outputs,
errors, reads and writes,

128
00:14:01.250 --> 00:14:06.875
side effects, limits, retry behavior,
approval, verification, and recovery

129
00:14:06.875 --> 00:14:12.500
contract. Draw the sequence from intent
through resolved target, preview,

130
00:14:12.500 --> 00:14:18.125
approval, call, postcondition, audit, and
completion. Test timeout after success,

131
00:14:18.125 --> 00:14:23.750
a duplicate request, and malicious
instructions inside tool output. For

132
00:14:23.750 --> 00:14:29.375
each, record stop, reconciliation,
compensation, rollback, disablement, or

133
00:14:29.375 --> 00:14:35.000
escalation behavior.

134
00:17:35.000 --> 00:17:38.571
When you are ready, begin the knowledge
check. You will

135
00:17:38.571 --> 00:17:42.143
identify a complete tool contract, respond
to a timeout after

136
00:17:42.143 --> 00:17:45.714
a payment request, classify instructions
inside tool output, distinguish a

137
00:17:45.714 --> 00:17:49.286
response from a verified postcondition,
and recognize an action requiring

138
00:17:49.286 --> 00:17:52.857
exact current approval. Review the class
or return to the

139
00:17:52.857 --> 00:17:56.429
activity before submitting. The assessment
begins only when you choose

140
00:17:56.429 --> 00:18:00.000
Begin knowledge check.

141
00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:05.000
Remember the boundary: tool access is
delegated authority. Resolve the

142
00:18:05.000 --> 00:18:10.000
target, expose consequence, approve
exactly, verify reality, and recover from

143
00:18:10.000 --> 00:18:15.000
recorded state.
